{"page":932,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.2516,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"918 Chapter 15: Multiple Integrals"},{"x":0.32414,"y":0.09376,"w":0.59311,"h":0.15091,"fs":0.01279,"n":10,"k":1,"t":"The moment of inertia of a shaft resembles in some ways the inertial mass of a loco- motive. To start a locomotive with mass m moving at a linear velocity y, we need to pro- vide a kinetic energy of KE = (1>2)my2. To stop the locomotive we have to remove this amount of energy. To start a shaft with moment of inertia I rotating at an angular velocity v, we need to provide a kinetic energy of KE = (1>2)Iv2. To stop the shaft we have to take this amount of energy back out. The shaft’s moment of inertia is analogous to the locomotive’s mass. What makes the locomotive hard to start or stop is its mass. What makes the shaft hard to start or stop is its moment of inertia. The moment of inertia depends not only on the mass of the shaft but also on its distribution. Mass that is farther away from the axis of rotation contributes more to the moment of inertia."},{"x":0.17014,"y":0.21313,"w":0.05272,"h":0.0146,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"\"x2 + y2"},{"x":0.32415,"y":0.24721,"w":0.59303,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"We now derive a formula for the moment of inertia for a solid in space. If r(x, y, z) is the distance from the point (x, y, z) in D to a line L, then the moment of inertia of the mass"},{"x":0.24845,"y":0.25256,"w":0.01454,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dV"},{"x":0.32496,"y":0.27724,"w":0.58569,"h":0.03033,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"∆mk = d(xk, yk, zk)∆Vk about the line L (as in Figure 15.38) is approximately ∆Ik = r2(xk, yk, zk)∆mk. The moment of inertia about L of the entire object is"},{"x":0.24808,"y":0.2779,"w":0.052,"h":0.0146,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"\"x2 + z2"},{"x":0.13629,"y":0.3017,"w":0.052,"h":0.0146,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"\"y2 + z2"},{"x":0.56869,"y":0.32638,"w":0.19687,"h":0.01608,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"r2(xk, yk, zk)d(xk, yk, zk) ∆Vk ="},{"x":0.8041,"y":0.32638,"w":0.04712,"h":0.01421,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"r2d dV."},{"x":0.37557,"y":0.32712,"w":0.0608,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"IL = lim"},{"x":0.46731,"y":0.32712,"w":0.07125,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"∆Ik = lim"},{"x":0.41106,"y":0.33127,"w":0.05087,"h":0.01664,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"nSq a"},{"x":0.51325,"y":0.33127,"w":0.05087,"h":0.01664,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"nSq a"},{"x":0.77291,"y":0.33863,"w":0.02905,"h":0.01952,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"l D"},{"x":0.44325,"y":0.3419,"w":0.02002,"h":0.00916,"fs":0.0087,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"k=1"},{"x":0.54545,"y":0.3419,"w":0.02002,"h":0.00916,"fs":0.0087,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"k=1"},{"x":0.32414,"y":0.36484,"w":0.36128,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"If L is the x-axis, then r2 = y2 + z2 (Figure 15.39) and"},{"x":0.04865,"y":0.3704,"w":0.26212,"h":0.02762,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 15.39 Distances from dV to the coordinate planes and axes."},{"x":0.57088,"y":0.39937,"w":0.15032,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01401,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(y2 + z2) d(x, y, z) dV."},{"x":0.50671,"y":0.40385,"w":0.0269,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Ix ="},{"x":0.54096,"y":0.41536,"w":0.02905,"h":0.01952,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"l D"},{"x":0.32414,"y":0.44288,"w":0.29098,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Similarly, if L is the y-axis or z-axis we have"},{"x":0.4214,"y":0.47227,"w":0.26061,"h":0.01982,"fs":0.01401,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"(x2 + z2) d(x, y, z) dV and Iz ="},{"x":0.71927,"y":0.47227,"w":0.1514,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01401,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(x2 + y2) d(x, y, z) dV."},{"x":0.35726,"y":0.47675,"w":0.02687,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Iy ="},{"x":0.39148,"y":0.48826,"w":0.02905,"h":0.01952,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"l D"},{"x":0.68935,"y":0.48826,"w":0.02905,"h":0.01952,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"l D"},{"x":0.32415,"y":0.5158,"w":0.593,"h":0.04348,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Table 15.2 summarize